virgl: a texture without a binding, and a sample count of one

Two things Gallium allows in a resource and the host's renderer (virglrenderer 0.10) refuses:
a texture with no binding at all, which it takes for a buffer ("Buffer bind flags require the
buffer target"), and a sample count of one for "not multisampled", which it takes for a
multisample texture and refuses outright on a buffer. The Direct3D 9 part creates both: its
read-back textures have no binding, and it writes one sample everywhere. With these the
resource was never made on the host, and what was read back from it was black.

Both are now put right where the resource goes to the host.

diff -ru a/src/gallium/drivers/virgl/virgl_resource.c b/src/gallium/drivers/virgl/virgl_resource.c
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/virgl/virgl_resource.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/virgl/virgl_resource.c
@@ -653,6 +653,15 @@
    vflags = pipe_to_virgl_flags(vs, templ->flags);
    virgl_resource_layout(&res->b, &res->metadata, 0, 0, 0, 0);
 
+   /* Two things Gallium allows and the host does not take. A texture with no binding at all
+    * (a staging texture to read back through): the host calls that a buffer and refuses it.
+    * And a sample count of one for "not multisampled": the host makes a multisample texture
+    * of any count above zero, and refuses a buffer with one.
+    */
+   if (templ->target != PIPE_BUFFER && vbind == 0)
+      vbind = VIRGL_BIND_SAMPLER_VIEW;
+   const unsigned host_samples = templ->nr_samples > 1 ? templ->nr_samples : 0;
+
    if ((vs->caps.caps.v2.capability_bits & VIRGL_CAP_APP_TWEAK_SUPPORT) &&
        vs->tweak_gles_emulate_bgra &&
       (templ->format == PIPE_FORMAT_B8G8R8A8_SRGB ||
@@ -680,7 +689,7 @@
                                           templ->depth0,
                                           templ->array_size,
                                           templ->last_level,
-                                          templ->nr_samples,
+                                          host_samples,
                                           vflags,
                                           alloc_size);
    if (!res->hw_res) {
